---------------------------------------------------------------------
PYTHON INSTALLATION
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1:

Press Windows + R

Type:

cmd

Press Enter.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 2:

Check whether Python is installed.

Type:

python --version

If Python is installed correctly, you will see:

Python 3.13.5

or

Python 3.10.0

---------------------------------------------------------------------

How to Check if Python is Added to PATH

Open Command Prompt.

Type:

python --version

Then type:

where python

If Python is added to PATH, its installation location will be displayed.

Example:

C:\Program Files\Python313\python.exe

---------------------------------------------------------------------
PYTHON LIBRARIES INSTALLATION
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1:

Open the GitHub Repository.

Example:

https://github.com/cmdAIML/12th_artificial_intelligence

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 2:

Download the Repository.

1. Click the green Code button.

2. Click Download ZIP.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 3:

Extract the ZIP file.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 4:

Open the extracted folder.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 5:

Double-click

install_libraries.bat

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 6:

Wait while Python installs all required libraries.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 7:

After installation you will see:

==================================================
Installation Completed Successfully!
==================================================

Press any key to continue...

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 8:

Verify Installation

Open Command Prompt.

Type:

python

Then type:

import numpy

import pandas

import matplotlib

If no error appears, all libraries are installed successfully.

Exit Python by typing:

exit()

---------------------------------------------------------------------
IF PYTHON IS NOT INSTALLED
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The installer will display:

Python is NOT installed or not added to PATH.

Please install Python from:

https://www.python.org/downloads/

During installation select:

☑ Add python.exe to PATH

Complete the installation.

Run install_libraries.bat again.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
CHECK INSTALLED LIBRARIES
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Open Command Prompt.

Type:

pip list

A list of all installed Python libraries will be displayed.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
VS CODE RUN BUTTON
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Install the Python extension in VS Code.

Open any Python (.py) file.

The Run ▶ button will appear in the top-right corner.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
UNIT 1
PYTHON PROGRAMMING-II
---------------------------------------------------------------------

WHAT IS A LIBRARY?

Definition:

A Python Library is a collection of pre-written code
(functions, classes and modules) that helps programmers
perform tasks without writing everything from scratch.

OR

A library is a collection of ready-made programs and
functions that make coding easier and faster.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY DO WE USE LIBRARIES?
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose you want to calculate the average marks
of 10,000 students.

Without a library, you would have to write a lot of code.

Using a library like NumPy, you can calculate
the average using only one function.

Libraries save:

• Time
• Effort
• Memory

---------------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE WITHOUT LIBRARY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose you want to add:

10 + 20 + 30 + 40 + 50

You have to write the logic yourself.

If there are 10,000 numbers,
the program becomes difficult.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
EXAMPLE WITH LIBRARY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

import numpy as np

numbers = [10,20,30,40,50]

print(np.mean(numbers))

Output:

30.0

Using only one function,
NumPy calculates the average.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
INTRODUCTION TO NUMPY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

NumPy stands for

Numerical Python

It is a Python library used to work with

• Numbers
• Arrays
• Mathematical Calculations

NumPy performs calculations quickly and efficiently.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTING NUMPY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax

import numpy as np

Explanation

import  -> Import a library

numpy   -> Library Name

as      -> Alias keyword

np      -> Short name of NumPy

---------------------------------------------------------------------
WHY DO WE USE NUMPY?
---------------------------------------------------------------------

• Performs calculations very fast.

• Handles large amounts of data.

• Provides many mathematical functions.

• Used in Data Science.

• Used in Artificial Intelligence.

• Used in Machine Learning.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
ADVANTAGES OF NUMPY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

• Faster than Python Lists.

• Uses less memory.

• Easy mathematical calculations.

• Easy to create arrays.

• Widely used in AI and ML.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT IS AN ARRAY?
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

An array is a data structure that stores
multiple values in a single variable.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
NUMPY ARRAY
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Definition

A NumPy Array is a collection of multiple
values stored in one variable.

It is used to store and process
numerical data efficiently.

Compared with Python Lists:

• Faster

• Less Memory

• More Mathematical Functions

---------------------------------------------------------------------
TYPES OF ARRAYS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

1. One Dimensional Array (1D)

Example

[10 20 30 40 50]

Contains only one row.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Two Dimensional Array (2D)

Example

[[10 20 30]
 [40 50 60]]

Contains rows and columns.

=====================================================================
END OF PART 1
=====================================================================
=====================================================================
PART 2
INDEXING, SLICING AND NUMPY OPERATIONS
=====================================================================

---------------------------------------------------------------------
INDEXING & SLICING
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Topics

• Positive Index

• Negative Index

• Array Slicing

---------------------------------------------------------------------
PROGRAM
---------------------------------------------------------------------

import numpy as np

arr = np.array([10,20,30,40,50])

print(arr[0])

print(arr[-1])

print(arr[1:4])

---------------------------------------------------------------------
OUTPUT
---------------------------------------------------------------------

10

50

[20 30 40]

---------------------------------------------------------------------
EXPLANATION
---------------------------------------------------------------------

arr[0]

Returns the first element.

Output

10

------------------------------------------------------------

arr[-1]

Returns the last element.

Output

50

------------------------------------------------------------

arr[1:4]

Returns elements from index 1 to index 3.

Output

[20 30 40]

---------------------------------------------------------------------
INDEX POSITION
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Array

[10   20   30   40   50]

Positive Index

 0    1    2    3    4

Negative Index

-5   -4   -3   -2   -1

---------------------------------------------------------------------
MATHEMATICAL OPERATIONS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

NumPy performs mathematical operations on arrays very easily.

The basic operations are

• Addition

• Subtraction

• Multiplication

• Division

=====================================================================
1. ADDITION
=====================================================================

Program

import numpy as np

a = np.array([1,2,3])

b = np.array([4,5,6])

print(a+b)

Output

[5 7 9]

Explanation

1 + 4 = 5

2 + 5 = 7

3 + 6 = 9

Each element is added with the element at the same position.

=====================================================================
2. SUBTRACTION
=====================================================================

Program

import numpy as np

a = np.array([10,20,30])

b = np.array([1,2,3])

print(a-b)

Output

[ 9 18 27]

Explanation

10 - 1 = 9

20 - 2 = 18

30 - 3 = 27

=====================================================================
3. MULTIPLICATION
=====================================================================

Program

import numpy as np

a = np.array([1,2,3])

b = np.array([4,5,6])

print(a*b)

Output

[ 4 10 18]

Explanation

1 × 4 = 4

2 × 5 = 10

3 × 6 = 18

=====================================================================
4. DIVISION
=====================================================================

Program

import numpy as np

a = np.array([10,20,30])

b = np.array([2,4,5])

print(a/b)

Output

[5. 5. 6.]

Explanation

10 ÷ 2 = 5

20 ÷ 4 = 5

30 ÷ 5 = 6

=====================================================================
STATISTICAL FUNCTIONS
=====================================================================

Statistical functions are used to analyse numerical data.

They help us find

• Average

• Middle Value

• Largest Value

• Smallest Value

• Total

• Standard Deviation

=====================================================================
1. mean()
=====================================================================

Definition

mean() returns the average of all numbers.

Program

import numpy as np

numbers = np.array([10,20,30,40])

print(np.mean(numbers))

Output

25.0

Explanation

10 + 20 + 30 + 40 = 100

100 ÷ 4 = 25

Mean = 25

=====================================================================
2. median()
=====================================================================

Definition

median() returns the middle value after arranging the numbers.

Program

import numpy as np

numbers = np.array([10,20,30,40,50])

print(np.median(numbers))

Output

30.0

Explanation

Numbers

10 20 30 40 50

Middle Value = 30

=====================================================================
3. max()
=====================================================================

Definition

max() returns the largest value.

Program

import numpy as np

numbers = np.array([10,20,30,40])

print(np.max(numbers))

Output

40

Explanation

40 is the largest value.

=====================================================================
4. min()
=====================================================================

Definition

min() returns the smallest value.

Program

import numpy as np

numbers = np.array([10,20,30,40])

print(np.min(numbers))

Output

10

Explanation

10 is the smallest value.

=====================================================================
5. sum()
=====================================================================

Definition

sum() returns the total of all values.

Program

import numpy as np

numbers = np.array([10,20,30,40])

print(np.sum(numbers))

Output

100

Explanation

10 + 20 + 30 + 40 = 100

Total = 100

